# Vayu — local development secrets.
#
# Copy this file to `.dev.vars` and fill in the values. `.dev.vars` is
# gitignored; this template is not, so it must never carry a real value.
#
#   cp .dev.vars.example .dev.vars
#
# THIS FILE IS FOR LOCAL DEVELOPMENT ONLY. `wrangler dev` reads it;
# `wrangler deploy` does NOT upload it. Production values are set per Worker:
#
#   npx wrangler secret put BETTER_AUTH_SECRET                        # storefront
#   npx wrangler secret put BETTER_AUTH_SECRET -c wrangler.api.jsonc  # api
#
# Which Worker needs which secret:
#
#   secret                     site   api   admin   payments
#   ------------------------------------------------------------
#   INTERNAL_SECRET             x      x      x
#   BETTER_AUTH_SECRET          x      x
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID            x      x
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET        x      x
#   RAZORPAY_KEY_ID                                    x
#   RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET                                x
#   RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET                            x
#
# PUBLIC_ORIGIN is not a secret — it is a public hostname — so in production
# it lives in the `vars` block of each wrangler config, not here.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The origin this site answers on. No trailing slash.
#
# Locally this is the wrangler dev address. In production it is the real
# domain, and it is the value people most often forget: it is the canonical
# URL on every product page, the <loc> in sitemap.xml, the Sitemap: line in
# robots.txt and the base of the Google OAuth callback.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
PUBLIC_ORIGIN=http://127.0.0.1:8787


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signs the customer session cookie (Better Auth).
#
# Any long random string. Changing it invalidates every signed-in session.
# Generate one with:  openssl rand -base64 32
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared between the storefront, the API and the admin Worker so they can
# authenticate each other's internal calls.
#
# MUST be byte-identical in all three. A mismatch is not a loud failure —
# internal calls simply start being rejected.
# Generate one with:  openssl rand -base64 32
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
INTERNAL_SECRET=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google sign-in. From console.cloud.google.com → APIs & Services →
# Credentials → OAuth 2.0 Client ID (type: Web application).
#
# The Authorised redirect URI must be registered there exactly as:
#   <PUBLIC_ORIGIN>/api/auth/callback/google
#
# That path is Better Auth's, and it is NOT the legacy
# /api/account/google/callback this file used to name. The button goes to
# /api/auth/sign-in/social (app/lib/account.js), Better Auth builds the
# redirect_uri from baseURL + basePath — PUBLIC_ORIGIN + /api/auth — and
# Google refuses anything not registered, with:
#
#   Error 400: redirect_uri_mismatch
#
# The local URI must match PUBLIC_ORIGIN above to the character, PORT
# INCLUDED: Better Auth sends the origin it is configured with, not the one
# the browser is on. Serving dev on 8791 with PUBLIC_ORIGIN on 8787 sends
# Google the 8787 callback and drops the shopper on a dead port.
#
# Add BOTH the local and the production URI, or sign-in works in one and
# fails in the other. Leave these blank to run without Google
# sign-in; email and password still work.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Razorpay. From dashboard.razorpay.com → Settings → API Keys.
#
# The key id is public — it is handed to the browser to open the checkout —
# and only the secret must be protected.
#
# These can also be set from the admin panel (Settings → Payment), which
# writes them to the D1 `settings` table. Prefer these variables: a database
# dump or admin access exposes the stored copy in plaintext, and the code
# reads the environment first and falls back to the row.
#
# The webhook secret is the one you type into Razorpay's webhook settings —
# it is what proves an incoming payment notification is genuinely theirs.
# Leave all three blank to run with cash on delivery only.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
RAZORPAY_KEY_ID=
RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET=
RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
